Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that breaks the list into two halves recursively until each portion contains a single element. After splitting it begins merging the pieces back while sorting them. This gives merge sort a consistent time complexity of O(n log n). It is stable and works well for large datasets. However it requires extra memory because it stores temporary arrays during merging. Merge sort handles linked lists efficiently because splitting and merging nodes is easier. Many real world systems use merge sort due to its predictable performance.